{"schema_version":1,"guid":"6ab8a823-21dd-4e08-9c05-cb4916bd2c3f","apple_collection_id":"1523090326","show":"The Ryan Leak Podcast","titel":"Feedback is Data, Not a Verdict","audio":{"url":"https://anchor.fm/s/297b36c0/podcast/play/124629574/https%3A%2F%2Fd3ctxlq1ktw2nl.cloudfront.net%2Fstaging%2F2026-7-23%2F430395088-44100-2-3a4e9275d388e.m4a","user_agent":"PodcastsAM/1.0 (+https://amapps.app)","groesse":11965801,"format":"m4a","geprueft_am":"2026-10-05T14:23:55+00:00"},"faehigkeiten":{"clips":false,"clips_aac":true,"clips_suche":false,"range":true,"kapitel":true,"dynamik":"unbestimmt","grund":"Dynamik weder belegt noch ausgeschlossen: keine Merkmale gefunden, aber eine unveraenderte Wiederholungsprobe beweist nichts; M4A: Ausschnitte nur als AAC-Bloecke (clips_aac) - eine App ohne ADTS-Umverpackung zeigt keine"},"einzeiler":"Why feedback feels like a judgment and how treating it as data fuels personal growth.","kurzfassung":"When receiving feedback, our brains often process it as a harsh personal verdict rather than useful information. This leads to pre-defensiveness, where we build rebuttals before even hearing the critique properly. By shifting to a growth posture, asking clarifying questions, and mining feedback for truth, we can avoid shutting down opportunities.","stichpunkte":["Feedback is frequently mistaken by the brain as a personal verdict rather than useful data","Pre-defending happens when someone builds a rebuttal in their head before a difficult conversation even starts","The person receiving feedback does not get to decide if they are being defensive; the other person decides","Asking follow-up questions like asking for elaboration helps prevent immediate emotional walls","The two extremes are total self-condemnation and extreme defensiveness, with curiosity being the sweet spot","Taking notes during feedback sessions shifts the focus away from winning an argument","Moving conversations from email to phone calls prevents misinterpreting tone and aggression","Not all feedback is accurate, as some stems from projection or jealousy, but filtering for gold is essential for growth"],"kapitel":[{"titel":"Feedback as Data, Not a Verdict","start":0.6},{"titel":"The Trap of Pre-Defending","start":123.92},{"titel":"Shifting to a Growth Posture","start":271.54},{"titel":"Real-World Application in Speaking Events","start":419.84},{"titel":"Mining Feedback for Gold","start":565.74}],"clips":[{"hook":"When feedback feels like a personal attack","start":37.361,"ende":57.516,"laenge":20.15,"byte_start":726277,"byte_ende":1048790,"bytes":322514,"range_header":"bytes=726277-1048790","vorlauf_s":0.0697,"inhalt_start":37.431,"anker_sha256":"2b4e1d0ec45701085c7530795c1cbb0940cda1f04e2a4a4d109d232f907d3f4e","anker_ende_sha256":"cbf32df4bb604440070c46ab8f7f58a86cb91649dbba4d4ea4199abcdaf202c5","einblendung_ms":60,"text":"Human brains tend to misinterpret constructive input as a negative personal evaluation.","aac":{"profil":2,"samplerate_index":4,"kanaele":2,"bloecke":[385,375,387,371,375,359,364,360,368,369,369,372,355,364,358,366,370,359,376,379,382,367,420,418,355,352,365,424,352,362,360,363,374,362,380,367,371,390,362,363,387,390,369,376,370,360,373,392,365,356,371,400,402,373,381,399,378,366,359,351,383,370,358,362,356,376,362,353,384,380,393,359,405,379,348,355,372,369,362,363,428,350,348,349,363,366,344,363,471,360,359,355,354,365,365,359,361,370,359,381,381,379,369,370,378,382,368,361,377,386,416,377,356,362,366,352,392,390,364,381,351,351,366,381,368,364,384,444,380,348,383,352,358,349,385,365,352,374,365,360,396,373,360,368,390,355,356,367,378,368,372,369,356,368,390,372,362,376,384,386,425,373,404,392,400,370,406,370,363,354,333,355,367,355,349,343,369,361,363,369,384,361,356,362,373,376,371,385,391,382,363,382,392,445,343,345,355,356,365,377,382,403,381,378,346,357,353,396,353,360,365,360,369,362,409,357,359,364,366,395,397,365,353,357,369,379,387,402,368,382,366,357,376,359,354,363,358,364,367,364,426,452,342,341,352,36